{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781240865,"nonce":"9NEYCGeoeTtVnAN/5f2T/IPZKNg=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABFPPmig","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/256","origin":"github"},"title":"Event-triggered video capture: ring-buffer cameras, save ±30 s around CA-gate stops","message":"## Goal\n\nEvent-triggered camera capture: cameras record continuously into a **RAM ring buffer** (nothing persisted), and when the CA safety gate trips, **±30 s of video around the event** is written to disk. Black-box/dashcam pattern: every e-stop self-documents, with no continuous-recording data burden.\n\n## Why\n\n- The 2026-06-12 gate mining (rolker/unh_marine_navigation#74 / #73 threshold work) hit exactly this gap: reflex-cloud + gate-state evidence exists for every stop, but visual ground truth ended 06-03 because `record_camera_topics.sh` is ad-hoc/manual and nobody ran it during the student-ops week. 1,568 GUIDED moving-STOP events on 06-08/06-09 have no imagery.\n- Continuous persistent recording is rejected on data-volume grounds (operator decision, 2026-06-11) — recording is manual *on purpose*. Event-triggered capture sidesteps that: ~11 MB per event (60 s, all four OAKs at observed ffmpeg rates) vs ~16 GB/day continuous.\n- Once the nav#74 persistence filter is tuned, the **surviving** stops are precisely the interesting ones (real obstacle or novel false-positive mode) — auto-capturing them builds the regression corpus for free.\n\n## Mechanism sketch\n\n- `rosbag2` **snapshot mode** (`ros2 bag record --snapshot-mode ...`): subscribes and buffers in RAM; writes only when the `~/snapshot` service is called.\n- Buffer the four OAK `image_raw/ffmpeg` + `segmentation` topics, plus the cheap context that makes replay self-contained: `collision_monitor/pointcloud`, `collision_monitor_state`, stop/slowdown polygons, `/bizzy/odom`, `cmd_vel` in/out, TF.\n- A small trigger node watches `collision_monitor_state`; on transition to STOP it schedules the snapshot call **+30 s later**, so a 60 s buffer yields [t−30, t+30].\n- **Debounce**: rate-limit to one snapshot per N minutes and/or only trigger on filtered (post-nav#74) stops — the current unfiltered gate fires hundreds of times per afternoon (mined 06-08/06-09 data), which would defeat the purpose.\n- **HEVC caveat**: a dumped buffer is only decodable from the first keyframe inside the window — either size the buffer with margin (e.g. 90 s for a 60 s usable window) or set the encoder keyframe interval accordingly. Verify decodability in the acceptance test.\n- Destination: `~/data/logs/bizzy_events/\u003ctimestamp\u003e_ca_stop/` (synced off-boat with the rest of `~/data/logs`).\n\n## Sequencing\n\nAfter the nav#74/#73 filter + stop-box tuning lands and is validated — the trigger rate must be sane first. Related: rolker/unh_marine_navigation#74, #73; complements #183 (operator awareness of CA events).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Fable 5`","files":null}]}